# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Reference observations (what became available)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

reference_observation:
  observer: "@claude"
  epistemic_status: observed
  observed_at: 2026-09-07
  what_became_available:
    - "Claude iOS app. The Effort sheet lists Low, Medium, High (Default), Extra, Max, Ultracode."
    - "The Ultracode row is a rounded band in deep indigo (roughly #2a1b4e fading to #1e1440) with white label text at left."
    - "Background layer: a grid of small square tiles (about 10 device pixels with 1 pixel gutters). Tiles never move; their brightness changes. A soft wave sweeps left to right and a radial bloom expands around the critter and fades, so the grid reads as a dithering pixel field that breathes."
    - "Foreground layer: an orange pixel critter (crab-like, holding a dark tool) at the right edge, mostly static, with two or three small yellow sparkle particles above it that change position each frame at roughly 8 to 12 frames per second."
    - "The same orange critter appears static at the top of the main screen above the greeting."
    - "Only the row that is highlighted animates; the rest of the sheet is still."
  observer_added:
    - "Two layers are separable: a scalar field driving tile brightness, and a sprite with a small particle overlay. This is the same split YAWN.bot already uses for its face transition (pixel field plus sprite)."
  remains_open:
    - "The implementation technology of the iOS band is not visible in a recording and no public source documents it; see technology.what_claude_uses."
  boundary: "This observation describes a third-party interface for study. It is not a license to copy Anthropic's mascot, and the YAWN.bot mascot remains the existing YAWN robot."

skin_reference_observation:
  observer: "@claude"
  epistemic_status: observed
  observed_at: 2026-09-07
  what_became_available:
    - "will-front-walk.png, will-back-walk.png (2048 x 256, eight 256 px frames each) and will-idle.png (1024 x 256, four frames) exist in the app under public/nestheads/characters/ and match the attached line-art walk cycle: round head, two dot eyes, black outline, white fill, a brown backpack on the front walk."
    - "yawn-front-walk.png, yawn-back-walk.png (eight frames) and yawn-idle.png (four frames) sit beside them with the same frame boxes and the same poses frame for frame: the robot has a CRT head with a sleepy screen face, a cyan and blue body, a pink neon outline, and a blue pack where the skin has a backpack."
    - "That gives twenty existing pairs (skin frame, robot frame) at 256 x 256: eight front walk, eight back walk, four idle."
    - "The dormant sprite shown on /new (YawnWorldBot) and in the global header is robot-blank-face-transparent.png (1254 x 1254): a browser-window-headed robot with the yawn.ai mark on its chest, rendered as a static image with a CSS bob when walking."
    - "The app already plays the robot sheets in Phaser (idle at 8 fps over 4 frames, walk at 20 fps over 8 frames) and has a CSS steps(4) idle fallback."
    - "The dormant browser-faced robot and the walking robot are different character designs: a rectangular bezelled monitor head on a neck, a boxy torso with the yawn.ai mark and two red buttons, and cylindrical limbs, versus Will's round head with an inset CRT window, a cyan body with a blue shadow half, and a navy pack. They share only the palette family."
  measured_by_research_agent:
    epistemic_status: reported
    findings:
      - "Same-index silhouette overlap (intersection over union at alpha above 64) is 0.994 to 0.996 on all twenty pairs; bounding boxes are identical to the pixel; each frame's best cross-match is its own index."
      - "The robot sheets use 23 opaque colors and hard one-bit alpha; the Will sheets use 583 colors with anti-aliased alpha. The robot outline is #ff2bcd and the body cyan is #1dddee, which are near but not equal to the brand tokens #fd49ac and #2fe3f4."
      - "Alpha-column detection on the robot sheets finds about 214 px visible cells inside the 256 px frames, so the sheets are uniform strips with transparent gutters."
  observer_added:
    - "The existing pairs are a translation dataset: same pose, same box, different identity and rendering style. The pipeline that made them is the pipeline to systematize."
    - "The identity fork between the dormant robot and the walking robot is a decision for Dave before any library-scale run: either the walking robot is the canonical skinned body and the dormant sprite is a separate sleeping asset, or one dormant frame is commissioned in the walking identity as the first approved job."
  remains_open:
    - "Which tool or model produced the existing robot sheets is not recorded in either repository; the receipts start with this contract."
    - "The full Will P library (cycles beyond walk and idle) is on Dave's machine and has not been inventoried."
    - "Which body is canonical for the header: the dormant design or the walking design."

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Coordinate, ownership, and provisioning
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

coordinate:
  house: yawn.bot/mascot
  states: yawn.bot/mascot/states
  skins: yawn.bot/mascot/skins
  owner_principal: principal:yawn
  owner_note: >
    The root YAWN.bot account owns the house; the mascot system is the root
    bot's body, not one person's avatar. The app currently resolves every
    non-handle yawn.bot coordinate to the site owner (Dave); a root principal
    handle is a known gap listed in open_lacunae.
  provisioned_viewers:
    - principal: principal:dave
      group: authenticated_owner
      grant: explicit-per-view
      may: [inspect, propose, approve-generation, approve-promotion, set-defaults, register-skin]
  public_viewers:
    group: public_anonymous
    may: [read-public-view, watch-public-state]
    may_not: [inspect-private-state, see-receipts, see-pending-candidates, change-defaults]
  fork_rule: >
    yawn.bot/mascot is the root bot's mascot house. A principal who yawns
    (forks) it receives {principal}/mascot with the same state map, manifest
    schema, and skin grammar; their sheets, accepted defaults, spend, and
    outputs belong to the fork owner. Forks inherit the state grammar, never
    the root's private state. This mirrors yawn.bot/image-generator, which
    forks to {principal}/image-generator with the style genome and operator
    contract but the fork owner's keys and outputs.
  provisioning_source: "yawn.bot src/domain/yawn-provisioning.ts groups and capabilities"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Layers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

layers:
  header_mark:
    where: "upper-left YAWN.bot mark in the global chrome on every route, including the public root"
    shows: "the current bot's mascot in its current state at 24 to 40 CSS pixels (the mark is 34 px inside a 40 px control today)"
    public_root_rule: "shows only the root bot's public state class; never a private coordinate's detail"
    accessible_name: "required; the state name is always exposed as text, never by motion or color alone"
    replaces: "the static robot-blank-face-transparent.png image rendered from createYawnFaceDefaultAvatarAssetPath"
    dormant_identity: "the browser-window-faced robot from /new stays the sleeping body; the walking robot sheet is the moving body"
  state_card:
    where: "section headers and profile images for a yawn or bot (128 to 256 CSS pixels)"
    shows: "foreground sprite over the background field, plus the state label and hard-gate badge"
  background_field:
    role: "state expression"
    form: "a grid of square cells whose brightness is driven by a per-state scalar field; cells never move"
    is_view: true
    may: [breathe, sweep, bloom, jitter, dissolve, resolve, drain]
    may_not: [carry state without a text label, write state, imply proof]
  foreground_sprite:
    role: "the body"
    identity: "the existing YAWN robot; canonical references are yawn-idle.png, yawn-front-walk.png, yawn-back-walk.png, and robot-blank-face-transparent.png"
    renders: "one accepted sprite sheet per state through the sprite manifest"
  skin:
    role: "an avatar option dressed over a yawn.bot"
    definition: "a complete base animation set (pose library) drawn once as a character; the reference skin is Will P"
    uses:
      - "as the pose source that the robot's own state sheets are translated from"
      - "as a selectable body a principal may dress over their bot (accepted preference), keeping the state grammar"
    rule: "a skin changes what the body looks like; it never changes what a state means or which state is shown"
  accessories:
    role: "lemmings-style overlays that make a swarm legible"
    kinds: [hat, badge, tool, bubble]
    semantics: "hats encode role (root_steward, objective_steward, worker); badges encode flags (needs_owner, error, local, unknown); tools encode the active move kind (terminal, pen, book, mail); bubbles carry one short text label"
    rule: "an accessory adds a visual role; it never remaps the canonical state and never replaces the text label; every accessory in a manifest carries a meaning string"
  world_swarm:
    where: "Phaser world and holarchy field views"
    shows: "many mascots, one per yawn or bot in view, each resolving its own state; children default to the same system"
    budget: "one canvas per view; sprites share textures; off-screen bodies do not animate"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. State model (a View over existing runtime state; never a store)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

state_model:
  schema_version: yawn.mascot-state.v0.1
  resolution_rule: >
    resolveMascotState(inputs) is a pure function. Precedence: hard-gate flags
    (error, blocked) outrank needs_owner, which outranks the loop status,
    which outranks micro-expressions from the mirror face. The function
    returns {state, variant, flags} and a replayable input hash. Unknown,
    missing, or stale inputs resolve to sleeping with an unknown badge; they
    are never guessed.
  inputs:
    loop_status: "src/domain/yawn-state-substrate.ts yawnLoopStatuses: sleeping | orienting | awaiting_choice | moving | awaiting_proof | updating | holding | closed (legacy dormant reads as sleeping)"
    worker_expression_state: "src/domain/automation-registry.ts AutomationWorkerExpressionState: sleeping | awake | working | needs_dave | blocked (from worker status draft | active | working | approval_required | blocked)"
    mirror_face_expression: "src/domain/yawn-frame-grid.ts YawnMirrorFaceExpressionState: idle | listening | thinking | proof_ready | blocked | asking | replaying"
    world_execution_state: "src/domain/world-coordinate-types.ts worldExecutionStates: idle | active | queued | running | waiting_context | waiting_approval | blocked | reviewing | done | error"
    world_actor_state: "src/components/brain-console/company-world-surface.tsx WorldActorState: active | working | approval_required | blocked | draft | ready"
    axis_statement_tone: "src/domain/yawn-quick-menu.ts YawnAxisStatementTone: ready | working | attention | blocked | proof | boundary | default"
    holarchy_lifecycle: "src/domain/yawn-holarchy-map.ts node lifecycle: root | proposed | active | accepted | unresolved | held | merged; action state: queued | active | waiting_for_proof | completed"
    coordinate_frame_status: "src/components/yawn-coordinate/yawn-coordinate-frame.tsx headerProvisioning.status: sleeping | draft | awake"
    header_status: "src/components/chrome/yawn-global-chrome.tsx registration.status.state: error | local | saved | working"
    inbox_status: "src/domain/yawn-bot-state.ts yawnBotInboxStatuses: new | claimed | answered | deferred | blocked_on_dave | handed_off | closed"
    objective_bot_lifecycle: "schemas/objective-holon.v0.1.schema.json lifecycleState: sleeping | orienting | active | waiting | blocked | paused | retired (protocol; not yet wired in the app)"
    move_kind: "the active move's kind when known (code, write, read, mail, world, none); no runtime enum supplies this yet, see open_lacunae"
    bot_role: "root_steward | objective_steward | worker"
    freshness: "age of the newest input; inputs older than the freshness window add the unknown badge"

  states:
    - state: sleeping
      mapped_from: [loop:sleeping, loop:dormant(legacy), worker:sleeping, execution:idle, face:idle, frame:sleeping, frame:draft, actor:draft, holarchy:proposed, bot:sleeping, worker_status:draft]
      foreground_animation: "idle breath, 4 frames at 8 fps (existing yawn-idle sheet); the header mark may use the browser-face dormant body"
      background_field: "still cells at low brightness with a slow breath (period about 500 ms, matching the idle breath period)"
      accessory_rule: "role hat only"
      public_safe: true
    - state: orienting
      mapped_from: [loop:orienting, worker:awake, execution:active, execution:queued, execution:waiting_context, face:listening, face:thinking, frame:awake, actor:active, holarchy_action:queued, bot:orienting]
      foreground_animation: "look-around: head turns, antenna scan (to be translated from the skin's look or idle-variant cycle)"
      background_field: "slow left-to-right sweep, cyan tint (observation and connection role)"
      accessory_rule: "role hat"
      public_safe: true
    - state: asking
      mapped_from: [loop:awaiting_choice, face:asking, inbox:new, inbox:claimed, tone:attention, holarchy:unresolved]
      foreground_animation: "holds up a question bubble; small bob (to be translated)"
      background_field: "cells pulse in place, lime tint (open or unresolved role)"
      accessory_rule: "bubble carries the one question label"
      public_safe: true
    - state: moving
      mapped_from: [loop:moving, worker:working, execution:running, header:working, actor:working, tone:working, holarchy_action:active, bot:active, move_kind:world]
      foreground_animation: "walk cycle, 8 frames at 20 fps (existing yawn-front-walk and yawn-back-walk sheets, translated from the skin walk cycle)"
      background_field: "faster sweep in the walking direction"
      accessory_rule: "tool overlay for the move kind"
      public_safe: true
    - state: working
      variant_of: moving
      selected_when: "move_kind is code, write, or read"
      mapped_from: [loop:moving, execution:running, move_kind:code, move_kind:write, move_kind:read]
      foreground_animation: "sits at a terminal; hands move; screen glyphs change (to be translated from the skin's sit or work cycle). Cells from the field stream into the screen as code."
      background_field: "cells drain toward the terminal and re-fill: the field is being turned into code"
      accessory_rule: "tool overlay: terminal, pen, or book"
      public_safe: true
    - state: proving
      mapped_from: [loop:awaiting_proof, execution:reviewing, face:proof_ready, tone:proof, holarchy_action:waiting_for_proof, bot:waiting]
      foreground_animation: "holds a magnifier over a receipt; nods (to be translated)"
      background_field: "radial bloom that expands from the sprite and settles; green tint (verified role) only after proof passes, neutral until then"
      accessory_rule: "badge: proof pending"
      public_safe: true
    - state: updating
      mapped_from: [loop:updating, face:replaying, execution:done, holarchy_action:completed, holarchy:accepted, actor:ready, tone:ready, header:saved]
      foreground_animation: "brief dissolve and re-resolve of the body (steps(7), reusing the face pixel-resolve timing)"
      background_field: "cells dissolve and re-resolve once, orange tint (source and provenance role)"
      accessory_rule: "none"
      public_safe: true
    - state: holding
      mapped_from: [loop:holding, worker_status:paused, inbox:deferred, holarchy:held, bot:paused, tone:boundary]
      foreground_animation: "sits down; slow blink (to be translated)"
      background_field: "very slow breath, dim"
      accessory_rule: "badge: held, with the hold reason as text in the provisioned view"
      public_safe: true
    - state: blocked
      mapped_from: [worker:blocked, execution:blocked, execution:error, face:blocked, inbox:blocked_on_dave, header:error, actor:blocked, tone:blocked, bot:blocked]
      foreground_animation: "stopped pose, arms crossed or looking at the viewer (to be translated)"
      background_field: "cells jitter at low amplitude, pink tint (unresolved lacuna role)"
      accessory_rule: "badge: blocked or error; the reason is always text"
      public_safe: true
      public_detail_rule: "the public view shows blocked without the reason"
    - state: closed
      mapped_from: [loop:closed, worker_status:retired, inbox:closed, inbox:handed_off, holarchy:merged, bot:retired]
      foreground_animation: "asleep with a small check mark; retired variant is desaturated (to be translated)"
      background_field: "static, no motion"
      accessory_rule: "badge: closed, retired, merged, or handed off"
      public_safe: true

  flags:
    needs_owner:
      mapped_from: [worker:needs_dave, execution:waiting_approval, inbox:blocked_on_dave, actor:approval_required, tone:attention]
      shows: "hat-level badge in the owner's color plus the text label needs owner"
      public_rule: "public view shows waiting, not who is needed"
    error:
      mapped_from: [execution:error, header:error]
      shows: "badge; the mascot state resolves to blocked"
    local_only:
      mapped_from: [header:local]
      shows: "small local badge; means browser-local draft state, no persistence"
    unknown_inputs:
      mapped_from: [missing input, stale input beyond the freshness window]
      shows: "unknown badge; state resolves to sleeping; the missing input is named in the provisioned view"

  invariants:
    - "The mascot state is derived; it is never written back to loop status, worker status, or any record."
    - "Every rendered state carries its text label and an accessible name; motion and color are never the only carrier."
    - "A public surface never derives a private detail from the animation (no reason strings, no coordinate names, no counts)."
    - "The same inputs produce the same state, variant, flags, and input hash on every device."
    - "Adding a new runtime status without a mapping fails the state-map test rather than silently rendering sleeping."
    - "Every animation transition corresponds to a state change with a source event; a transition with no source event is a test failure."

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Manifest (extends the sprite manifest the runtime already detects)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

manifest:
  schema_version: yawn.mascot-manifest.v0.1
  extends: "nestheads.sprite-sheet.v1 (src/domain/sprite-sheets.ts): alpha-column frame detection, frameBox, frames, fps, loop, pivot; detection confidence 0.96 for uniform frames"
  shape:
    manifest_id: "mascot:{owner}:{revision}"
    owner_principal: principal
    identity_reference_sheets: [sha256]
    sizes:
      - { id: icon, frame_px: 32, targets: [24, 32, 40] }
      - { id: card, frame_px: 128, targets: [96, 128, 160] }
      - { id: world, frame_px: 256, targets: [176, 256] }
    pivot: { x: 0.5, y: 0.86 }
    palette: "brand tokens from interface/yawn-brand-v1.yawn plus the character's own body colors"
    states:
      "{state}":
        sheets:
          "{size}": { path: string, sha256: string, sprite_manifest: nestheads.sprite-sheet.v1 }
        fps: number
        loop: boolean
        reduced_motion_frame: index
        transitions_in: [{ from: state, frames: optional short bridge sheet }]
        field: { pattern: still|breath|sweep|pulse|bloom|drain|dissolve|jitter, period_ms: number, tint_role: brand semantic role }
        provenance:
          skin_id: string
          source_frame_hashes: [sha256]
          brief_hash: sha256
          approval_receipt: record ref
          generation_receipt: record ref
          promotion_receipt: record ref
    skins:
      "{skin_id}":
        label: text
        source_sheets: [{ cycle: string, path: string, sha256: string, frames: number, fps: number }]
        pose_keypoints: optional per-frame keypoints or silhouettes
        translated_sheets: [{ state: string, size: string, sha256: string, pairing_receipt: record ref }]
        rights: "who holds the character rights and whether translation and publication are licensed"
    accessories:
      "{kind}:{name}": { sheet: path, sha256: string, anchor: { x: number, y: number }, meaning: text }
    defaults_scope: "principal | arena | yawn (accepted projection-preference records, schemas/projection-preference.v1.schema.json, viewKind mascot)"
  rules:
    - "A state with no accepted sheet falls back to the nearest accepted ancestor state (working -> moving -> sleeping) and shows a badge saying which sheet is missing in the provisioned view."
    - "Sheets are authored or generated size-native (32, 128, 256); a 256 px sheet is never nearest-neighbor downscaled to 32 px for the header mark."
    - "Each sheet is content-addressed; changing pixels changes the hash and requires a new promotion receipt."
    - "A skin's translated sheets are stored per state and size like the root body; dressing a skin is an accepted preference that swaps the body layer only."
    - "The manifest is a View input. It carries no authority and cannot change what a state means."
    - "Root-bot sheets that the header mark needs on every page are served as static public assets, not short-lived signed URLs."
    - "Generated frames land in fixed cells; a strip whose detected cell count differs from the brief fails detection rather than being trusted."

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Technology
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

technology:
  what_claude_uses:
    - surface: "Claude Code CLI (npm package @anthropic-ai/claude-code 2.1.263, inspected in the shipped binary)"
      epistemic_status: observed
      finding: >
        The terminal UI is a React renderer derived from Ink with Yoga layout
        that paints ANSI truecolor text cells. The mascot (called Clawd in the
        code) is a 9 column by 3 row grid of Unicode block and quadrant glyphs
        colored rgb(215,119,87) on black, with four poses (default, look-left,
        look-right, arms-up) and entrance sequences (skip, jump, look, spin,
        celebrate) stepped by a timer at 60 ms per frame, disabled under a
        reduced-motion setting. The ultracode effort row is a component named
        UltraRippleText: each character's background color comes from a radial
        cosine wave over an eight-step violet ramp from rgb(62,22,118) to
        rgb(140,80,240), wavelength 20 cells, ticking every 80 ms at 0.03
        cells per millisecond, label color #d0b4ff. No dither, shader, sprite,
        or image asset is involved; ultracode itself is a session setting, not
        a visual mode.
      source: "package tarball and platform binary inspected on 2026-09-07; https://code.claude.com/docs/en/model-config"
    - surface: "Claude iOS app (the recording)"
      epistemic_status: unknown
      finding: >
        No public source documents the band's implementation. The visible
        grammar matches the CLI ripple: a deep-violet to bright-violet wave on
        a coarse cell grid plus the same crab figure rendered as pixels. A
        SwiftUI timeline-driven canvas, a Metal color effect, or a baked asset
        are all consistent with the pixels; none is documented.
      source: "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/remote-control (confirms only that effort and ultracode are settable from a phone)"
    - surface: "claude.ai web"
      epistemic_status: reported
      finding: >
        Not inspectable without a session (the site answers unauthenticated
        fetches with a challenge page). A May 2026 write-up recreated four of
        the web mascot animations with SVG and GSAP frame sequencing; it does
        not state the original format.
      source: "https://tympanus.net/codrops/2026/05/05/reverse-engineering-claude-ais-mascot-animations-with-svg-and-gsap/"
    - surface: "Claude desktop app"
      epistemic_status: reported
      finding: "An Electron shell hosting the Code tab; its effort picker sends the ultracode setting and no animated band is documented."
      source: "https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/66083"
  takeaway: >
    The reference look is a scalar field over a coarse cell grid plus a tiny
    pose grid, not an asset-heavy shader pipeline. The same split fits the
    web: cells whose brightness follows a wave, a small sprite on top, text
    labels beside both.

  recommended_per_layer:
    - layer: header_mark
      technology: "CSS sprite strip: a span with role img and an accessible state name, an inner strip translated by steps(N) (compositor-only), image-rendering pixelated, data-mascot-state attribute, keyframes generated from the manifest; the Web Animations API for one-shot transitions"
      why: "renders in server HTML with the right first frame, zero JavaScript on the hot path, no WebGL context, honors prefers-reduced-motion by freezing on the reduced_motion_frame"
      epistemic_status: inferred
    - layer: state_card
      technology: "the same CSS sprite with layered strips (body, hat, accessory, fx) bound to one duration so overlays stay in sync; escalate to a Canvas 2D player on the shared ticker only when tinting or per-instance composition is needed"
      why: "128 and 256 px sheets already exist; layering keeps accessories in phase without a runtime"
      epistemic_status: inferred
    - layer: background_field
      technology: "Canvas 2D low-resolution cell buffer (about 96 x 12 cells for a header band, 64 x 64 for a card) updated at 8 to 12 fps by the shared ticker: a cosine wave plus a Bayer threshold and slow drift, parameterized by state (tint role, density, speed, direction), drawn with imageSmoothingEnabled false and image-rendering pixelated; a CSS gradient and steps() mask fallback for no-JavaScript and reduced motion (the face transition already does this); the same parameters as a fragment shader only inside the Phaser world"
      why: "one small buffer is cheap on phones, needs no WebGL, and reproduces the observed grammar exactly"
      epistemic_status: inferred
    - layer: world_swarm
      technology: "exactly one canvas per view: Phaser 4 sprites (pixelArt true, roundPixels true) on the existing WebGL context inside the world, a sprite GPU layer above about 200 bodies; a single Canvas 2D batch renderer outside the world; never a WebGL context or canvas per bot"
      why: "WebKit caps active WebGL contexts at 16 per page and recycles the oldest; Safari does not apply image-rendering pixelated to WebGL canvases, so Phaser relies on nearest textures and integer resolution"
      epistemic_status: reported
    - layer: shared_driver
      technology: "one MascotStateStore (useSyncExternalStore) keyed by yawn id, one ticker throttled to manifest fps, pause on document.hidden and off-screen, reduced-motion stills, a persisted user pause toggle proposed for the chrome"
      why: "React props and realtime updates write to one place; every renderer subscribes; WCAG 2.2.2 wants a pause control for loops longer than five seconds"
      epistemic_status: inferred
  not_chosen:
    - "PixiJS: a second WebGL runtime beside Phaser with no pixel advantage"
    - "Rive and dotLottie: vector runtimes with 345 to 876 KB gzipped WebAssembly payloads and no nearest-neighbor concept"
    - "animated image formats (APNG, WebP, AVIF): cannot be paused, seeked, or state-driven; use only for exported previews (email, Slack, Open Graph)"
  constraints:
    - "prefers-reduced-motion shows the reduced_motion_frame and a still field"
    - "hidden tabs and off-screen bodies stop their tickers; CSS animations pause inside content-visibility skipped subtrees"
    - "image-rendering pixelated (with crisp-edges as the fallback declaration) on every raster layer"
    - "the header mark never holds a WebGL context; WebKit limits active contexts to 16 per page"
    - "iOS caps total canvas backing memory (about 224 to 384 MB); reuse a few shared canvases"
    - "sheets are size-native; the 32 px icon sheet is generated from the accepted 256 px sheet as a reference, never scaled from it"
    - "the frame-consistency validator (uniform frame width, pivot drift under 1 px, alpha-column count equal to the brief) runs before any sheet is promoted"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. Skin system and translation
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

skin_system:
  definition: >
    A skin is a complete base animation set drawn once as a character. Its
    frames are a pose library. The foundational YAWN.bot sheets are made by
    translating each skin frame into the robot identity while preserving the
    pose, the frame box, and the pivot. A translated skin can also be dressed
    over a bot as an avatar option. The reference skin is Will P (Nestheads
    Inc); the dormant body is the browser-faced robot from /new and the
    header; the working body is the walking robot sheet.
  base_pose_library:
    skin_id: will-p
    known_cycles: [front-walk (8), back-walk (8), idle (4)]
    reported_cycles: "the full Will P animation set on Dave's machine, not yet inventoried"
    frame_box: 256x256
    style: "black outline, white fill, round head, two dot eyes, transparent background"
  existing_pairs:
    count: 20
    pairs:
      - { cycle: front-walk, skin: public/nestheads/characters/will-front-walk.png, robot: public/nestheads/characters/yawn-front-walk.png, frames: 8, fps: 20 }
      - { cycle: back-walk, skin: public/nestheads/characters/will-back-walk.png, robot: public/nestheads/characters/yawn-back-walk.png, frames: 8, fps: 20 }
      - { cycle: idle, skin: public/nestheads/characters/will-idle.png, robot: public/nestheads/characters/yawn-idle.png, frames: 4, fps: 8 }
    role: "the first training and evaluation set for pose-preserving translation"
  translation_contract:
    input: "one skin frame (exact pose) + the robot identity references + the brand palette + the frame box and pivot"
    output: "one robot frame in the same pose, same box, transparent background, palette-locked"
    consistency: "translate a whole cycle as one job with a fixed seed where the provider returns one, an anchor frame first, then the remaining frames with the anchor as an additional reference"
    scoring:
      pose_fidelity: "silhouette intersection over union against the skin frame (ground truth on the existing pairs is 0.995; review gate at or above 0.95), bounding box equality, and edge chamfer distance; skeleton keypoints are secondary because pose detectors are unreliable on round-headed cartoons"
      identity: "embedding similarity (DINOv2-class, on alpha-masked crops) against the accepted robot frames"
      grid_fidelity: "palette-lock delta and pixel-grid detection confidence after post-processing"
      rule: "scores are receipt fields for review; they never approve a sheet"
      frame_rule: "generate each frame into its own fixed 256 x 256 box; assemble the strip from known cells rather than relying on alpha-column detection of a generated strip"
  lanes:
    - lane: hosted-frontier-edit
      tools: "OpenAI gpt-image-2 edits with the skin frame as the edited image, up to 16 reference images, background transparent (preview) and png output; Google Nano Banana 2 as an alternate (no alpha output, no seed)"
      cost: "about USD 0.10 to 0.20 per frame when the edited skin frame, two to four in-context pairs, and the robot sheet are attached as references (the model bills image input tokens); no seed is returned, so receipts carry the request id, usage, and output hash"
      fit: "fastest way to a first translated cycle; identity conditioning is strong; pixel grid must be fixed in post; one approved probe cycle must return real usage before the per-frame cost is trusted"
      epistemic_status: reported
    - lane: local-deterministic
      tools: "FLUX.2 Klein base with open reference-control adapters (lineart or canny map from the skin frame as the structure input, the robot frame as the identity input) plus a paired edit adapter trained on the existing pairs with ai-toolkit, run through the app's loopback ComfyUI adapter. Klein 4B is Apache-2.0 but today has only a depth control adapter; Klein 9B has lineart and canny adapters but sits under a non-commercial license that needs a legal read for an internal loopback"
      cost: "no per-frame provider cost after training; about USD 9 to 17 per hosted training run of 1000 to 2000 steps, or about one hour on a 24 GB local GPU with quantization; roughly 35 seconds per frame on a current consumer GPU"
      fit: "seed-returning, reproducible, the lane the art engine already plans, and the only lane whose marginal cost stays near zero across a holarchy of bots and states"
      epistemic_status: reported
    - lane: pixel-native
      tools: "Retro Diffusion image-to-image with an input palette, background removal, and a seed; PixelLab animate-with-skeleton for poses the skin lacks"
      cost: "about USD 0.03 to 0.25 per call"
      fit: "grid-true output with fewer cleanup steps; commercial terms must be read by Dave before adoption"
      epistemic_status: reported
    - lane: video-driven
      tools: "Wan 2.2 Animate (Apache-2.0, local) driving the robot keyframe with a motion video; hosted editors such as Runway Aleph, Kling, or Veo as alternates; never Sora 2 (API removed 2026-09-24)"
      cost: "about USD 0.60 to 1.50 per short clip hosted with minimum durations; identity drift, missing alpha, and loop drift are the real cost"
      fit: "not recommended for eight-frame loops; backlog only for authoring a new pose cycle when the skin library lacks a state"
      epistemic_status: reported
  primary_recommendation: "hosted-frontier-edit for the first translated cycles (one approved probe cycle first, compared against the 0.995 ground truth), local-deterministic as the system of record once the paired-frame adapter is trained and reviewed; retrain after every five to ten approved cycles so the local model absorbs the library"
  recommendation_status: inferred
  training_plan:
    dataset: "the 20 existing pairs now; the art engine's registry targets 20 to 40 curated images; every accepted translation adds a pair"
    method: "a paired edit adapter trained on (skin frame as control, robot frame as target) pairs with ai-toolkit's control-directory layout and captions naming the state and direction, layered over an open reference-control adapter so structure comes from the skin frame and only identity and rendering are learned; config checked in; seeds, steps, and dataset hashes in the receipt; a leave-one-cycle-out evaluation gates publication on pose overlap at or above 0.95 and identity similarity at or above the hand-made sheets"
    when_worth_it: "when the pipeline must be self-hosted, seed-reproducible, and license-clean, or when holarchy variants (hats, accessories) multiply; reference conditioning alone is enough for one mascot's identity"
    status: planned-approval-gated
    epistemic_status: inferred
  costs:
    one_cycle_eight_frames: "about USD 0.80 to 1.60 on the primary hosted lane (ceiling USD 2 per cycle), about USD 0.60 on a seeded multi-reference edit fallback; near zero marginal on the local lane"
    library_forty_cycles: "about USD 32 to 64 in primary hosted calls, budgeted at three times that for rerolls (ceiling USD 200), plus review time; about USD 24 on the fallback; one training run of about USD 9 to 17 for the local lane"
    epistemic_status: inferred
    note: "plus or minus half until one approved probe cycle returns real usage; the forty-cycle count is a placeholder until the Will P library is inventoried"
  rights:
    - "Will P is Nestheads Inc character art; a skin record names its rights holder and whether translation and publication are licensed."
    - "Third-party skins are never translated without a rights statement in the skin record."
    - "Model licenses are part of the lane record: the non-commercial license on the larger Klein base needs a legal read before an internal loopback uses it; the 4B base is Apache-2.0."
  palette_reconciliation: "the robot sheets' outline and body colors are near but not equal to the brand tokens; the skin manifest declares the sheet palette explicitly and the manifest records whether a sheet is brand-token-locked or sheet-palette-locked"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. Generation pipeline (approval-gated; reuses the image-generator idiom)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

generation_pipeline:
  reuses:
    - "yawn.bot/image-generator: one candidate per explicit owner press, dry-run receipt with the exact prompt and spend estimate (src/lib/yawn-image/generate.ts, gpt-image-1 edits with the two house references)"
    - "checked-in style contract (src/lib/yawn-image/style-contract.ts) and house reference images"
    - "art brief and local candidate contracts (schemas/art-brief.v1.schema.json, schemas/local-art-candidate.v1.schema.json, interface/local-observation-art-v0.1.yawn)"
    - "owner-decision receipt binding by canonical JSON sha256 (records/interception-statement-image-approval-2026-08-29.yawn)"
    - "nestheads-art-engine model and LoRA registries, keyframe extraction and LoRA training plans (approval-gated)"
    - "sprite_sheet media assets and alpha-column detection (src/app/api/nestheads/sprite-sheets/route.ts, src/domain/sprite-sheets.ts)"
    - "YawnAvatarStudioModal asset packets with paid_generation approval requests (src/components/inspector/yawn-avatar-studio-modal.tsx)"
  stages:
    - stage: reference_pack
      input: "robot identity references (yawn-idle, yawn-front-walk, yawn-back-walk, robot-blank-face-transparent) plus the skin's cycles"
      output: "content-addressed pack with per-frame hashes, frame box, pivot, and the pairs already known"
      approval_required: false
    - stage: pose_pack
      input: "one skin cycle"
      output: "per-frame silhouettes and optional keypoints, frame box, pivot, anchor rule (bottom-center for walks, centroid for idles)"
      approval_required: false
    - stage: translation_brief
      input: "one state from the state model, the target cycle, the reference pack, the brand palette, frame count, fps, size, negative constraints"
      output: "deterministic brief hash (canonical JSON, sha256, explicit seed or seed unsupported) per cycle and size"
      approval_required: false
    - stage: dry_run
      output: "exact prompt, lane, provider, candidate count, spend estimate; no call"
      approval_required: false
    - stage: owner_approval
      output: "owner-decision receipt naming the brief hash, lane and adapter, candidate count, spend ceiling, expiry; one receipt may cover one bounded batch (one cycle or one state at one size), never standing authority"
      approval_required: true
    - stage: translate_frames
      output: "candidate frames with provider request id, model snapshot, seed when returned, usage, input hashes, output sha256 per frame"
      approval_required: true
    - stage: post_process
      output: "background removed (birefnet-general, never a model with a paid-only commercial term), palette quantized to the brand palette plus body colors, nearest-neighbor downscale to the frame box, frames aligned on the anchor, 2 px transparent gutters, one horizontal strip that alpha-column detection reads"
      approval_required: false
      determinism: "same input hashes and parameters produce the same output hash; the post-processing version hash is a receipt field"
    - stage: detect_and_score
      output: "nestheads.sprite-sheet.v1 manifest with detection confidence, pose fidelity, identity, and grid fidelity scores"
      approval_required: false
    - stage: review
      output: "side-by-side against the skin cycle and the robot references in DOM and Phaser; accept, reject, or revise; a hand fix in Aseprite with a deterministic CLI re-export is allowed and hashed"
      approval_required: true
    - stage: promote
      output: "append-only promotion receipt binding sheet sha256 to a state slot, size, and skin id in the mascot manifest"
      approval_required: true
    - stage: publish
      output: "the manifest revision the header mark and world read; root-bot sheets as static public assets"
      approval_required: true
  receipt_fields:
    - lane and provider adapter
    - model snapshot
    - seed or seed-unsupported
    - provider request id
    - usage tokens or balance cost, and actual cost when known
    - sha256 of every input reference and skin frame
    - sha256 of every output frame and the packed strip
    - post-processing version hash (palette id, downscale kernel, anchor rule)
    - detection confidence and the three scores
  training_lane:
    status: planned-approval-gated
    reason: "the art engine already plans a core style LoRA on a 20 to 40 image curated set; the paired frames are that dataset's seed"
    blocked_without_approval: [training_job, adapter_publish, production_release]
  approvals_before_spend:
    - any paid image or video call (OpenAI, Google, Black Forest Labs managed, Retro Diffusion, PixelLab)
    - any training job, adapter publish, or production release
    - any media_assets write, manifest replacement, or state-map change
    - enabling a new provider string or a preview feature in the receipt union
    - accepting a provider's output license after Dave reads its terms
    - confirming rights for any skin or driving video before translation
    - a legal read on the base model license for the local lane and on the Will P skin license for derivative robot sheets
    - deciding the canonical body (walking robot versus dormant robot) and the palette lock before a library-scale run
    - adding a new house reference image
  boundaries:
    - "no provider call, spend, storage write, or publication without a current receipt naming this brief hash"
    - "a nonconforming candidate ends its grant; it does not authorize another"
    - "generated frames are candidates; acceptance, promotion, and publication are separate receipts"
    - "the character identity is the existing YAWN robot; a candidate that drifts identity fails review"
    - "raw private source never enters a renderer prompt"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 10. Views
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

views:
  public_view:
    route: /mascot
    class: public_view
    chrome: "interface/yawn-chrome-v1.yawn public_view geometry"
    format: "the /frontier pattern: coordinate line, one governing question, one honesty line, a ranked list of native details/summary entries with only the top entry open, a boundary line; zero JavaScript required"
    governing_question: "What is the YAWN.bot mascot?"
    shows:
      - "the mascot in its public state with the state name as text"
      - "the ranked questions marked public_safe with their public answers"
      - "the state list with names and one still frame each"
    hides:
      - "receipts, pending candidates, spend, reasons behind blocked or held, private coordinates, skin source libraries"
    fallback: "no motion: still frame and text"
    pattern_source: "yawn.bot src/app/frontier/page.tsx with src/components/cognitive-coordinate/question-first-list.tsx"
  provisioned_view:
    route: /mascot
    class: member
    viewer: "principal:dave (or a fork owner on their fork)"
    pattern_source: "yawn.bot src/app/dave/page.tsx: session decides the public mission or the owner surface on one route, with projectCoordinateRecordAccess supplying the view list"
    shows:
      - "everything in the public view"
      - "the full stack-ranked question set with answers, the case against each answer, the proof that would settle it, and its loop status"
      - "the live state resolution: inputs, resolved state, variant, flags, input hash, freshness"
      - "the manifest inspector: which states have accepted sheets at which sizes, which fall back, hashes, receipts"
      - "translation proposals awaiting approval with dry-run cost"
      - "accepted defaults and their scope"
    rule: "the provisioned extras render from a server component behind the session check, never by hiding client-side"
  states_view:
    route: /mascot/states
    class: member
    shows:
      - "one row per state: mapped inputs, current sheet per size, field pattern, accessory rules"
      - "controls to propose a different accepted sheet or field for a state at principal, arena, or yawn scope"
    rule: "a change here is an accepted projection preference; it never changes what a state means or what the runtime does"
  skins_view:
    route: /mascot/skins
    class: member
    shows:
      - "registered skins with rights, source cycles, translated states, and pairing receipts"
      - "the pose library per skin and which states still lack a translated cycle"
      - "dress this skin over a bot at principal, arena, or yawn scope (accepted preference)"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 11. Ranked questions (the FAQ; loops stay visible)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

ranked_questions:
  packet: question-packets/mascot.yawn
  format: "rank, key, question, answer, epistemic_status, case_against, proof, loop_status, public_safe, links"
  rule: "answers carry their status; proof, not the answer, upgrades it"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 12. Boundaries
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

boundaries:
  must:
    - "render the state name as accessible text with every animation"
    - "resolve state from runtime inputs through the one state map"
    - "respect prefers-reduced-motion and hidden-tab pausing"
    - "keep public surfaces free of private detail"
    - "bind every generated sheet to a brief hash, approval receipt, and promotion receipt"
    - "name the rights holder of every skin before translating it"
  must_not:
    - "let the mascot write, ratify, or imply any state"
    - "let a projection preference or a skin remap a canonical state"
    - "generate, spend, store, train, or publish without a current receipt"
    - "copy a third-party mascot; the body stays the YAWN robot or a rights-cleared skin"
    - "treat model confidence, a fidelity score, or visual quality as approval"
    - "carry state by color or motion alone"
    - "hold a WebGL context in the header mark or one canvas per bot"
  AI_default:
    may: [observe, inventory skins, compile briefs, dry-run, propose sheets, propose state maps, score candidates, test within grant]
    may_not: [self-authorize generation, promote a candidate, change defaults, widen provisioning, start training]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 13. Proof
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

proof:
  acceptance:
    - "The state-map test covers every value of every input enum; adding an unmapped value fails."
    - "For each state, the header mark renders the accepted or fallback sheet with a text label and an accessible name."
    - "With prefers-reduced-motion, every layer shows its reduced_motion_frame and no ticker runs."
    - "The public root header shows the root bot's public state only; a signed-out Playwright run finds no private detail in the DOM."
    - "A manifest with a tampered sheet hash fails validation and falls back."
    - "A generation, translation, or training attempt without a current receipt is refused and leaves an inspectable refusal record."
    - "One translated cycle travels reference pack, pose pack, brief hash, approval receipt, candidate, post-process, manifest, review, promotion receipt with every hash reconciling."
    - "Route-owned browser console errors are zero on /mascot, /mascot/states, and /mascot/skins."
    - "A swarm of 200 bodies in the world view keeps one canvas and does not animate off-screen bodies."
    - "A throttled mobile profile keeps main-thread time per frame within budget with 50 bodies visible."
  falsifiers:
    - "The mascot displays a state that the state map did not derive from runtime inputs."
    - "Two devices resolve different states from the same inputs."
    - "A public visitor can read a blocked reason, a receipt, a skin source path, or a private coordinate from the mascot surface."
    - "A generated sheet reaches a state slot without a promotion receipt."
    - "A translated frame passes review with a pose that does not match its skin frame."
  verifier:
    - "unit tests for the state map, manifest, and skin pairing"
    - "Playwright receipts for public and provisioned /mascot"
    - "hash reconciliation between briefs, receipts, and published sheets"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 14. Implementation projection (where this lands in YAWN.bot)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

implementation_projection:
  repository: yawn-ai/yawn.bot
  integration_points:
    - "src/domain/mascot-state.ts (new): resolveMascotState over every input named in state_model.inputs, with the exhaustive enum test"
    - "src/domain/mascot-manifest.ts (new): yawn.mascot-manifest.v0.1 over nestheads.sprite-sheet.v1, a resolver (state, size, layer) -> sheet with ancestor fallback, a build-time CSS keyframe generator, a Canvas 2D frame player, and a Phaser anims adapter"
    - "src/domain/mascot-defaults.ts (new): nearest-ancestor resolver for accepted projection preferences with viewKind mascot (YAWNS/Policies/YAWN_HOLARCHY_INHERITANCE.yawn rules)"
    - "src/components/chrome/yawn-global-chrome.tsx: add a brandVisual slot to the brand action and header props, and replace the three static brandImage sites (lines 1116 to 1150 at audit time) with a YawnMascotMark client component that falls back to the image"
    - "src/components/bot/yawn-world-bot.tsx: the /new world bot reads the same state store instead of a walking boolean"
    - "src/components/home/yawn-consent-avatar-runtime.tsx and src/components/brain-console/company-world-surface.tsx: replace the local executionState -> working|idle mapping with resolveMascotState"
    - "src/app/mascot/page.tsx, src/app/mascot/states/page.tsx, src/app/mascot/skins/page.tsx (new): frontier-style public view; provisioned views behind the session check following src/app/dave/page.tsx"
    - "src/lib/yawn-image: add a translate-frames adapter beside generateYawnImage with the same receipt idiom, extended by the receipt_fields above"
    - "nestheads-art-engine: register the reference pack and the 20 pairs as the core style dataset seed; add the paired-frame LoRA plan to the LoRA registry"
    - "supabase media_assets kind sprite_sheet: metadata {mascotState, size, skinId, manifestRevision, briefSha256, promotionReceipt}"
    - "protocol/dot-yawn.lock.json: vendor interface/yawn-mascot-v0.1.yawn and question-packets/mascot.yawn once accepted"
  first_move:
    smallest_reversible_step: >
      Ship the state map and the header mark using only the existing sheets
      (sleeping from yawn-idle or the browser-face body, moving from
      yawn-front-walk) with the CSS field band, behind a route flag. No
      generation, no spend. Then translate one cycle (the skin's idle or
      sit cycle into the working state) through the full receipt chain to
      prove the pipeline before the rest of the library.
    proof: "state-map test green; header mark visible with text label; reduced-motion still; public root unchanged for private detail; one promoted sheet with a reconciled receipt chain"

open_lacunae:
  - "Which existing runtime field supplies move_kind (thread kind, work order type, or a new field on the bot state) so the working variant can be selected."
  - "A root principal handle for principal:yawn, since coordinate access currently resolves non-handle coordinates to the site owner."
  - "Whether the dashboard's idle fps of 4 or the motion constant of 8 is canonical for sleeping."
  - "The full inventory of Will P cycles and which state each maps to."
  - "Whether the dormant header robot is re-authored in the walking robot's identity or kept as a separate sleeping design."
  - "How many reference images the hosted editor accepts per call (sources disagree between ten and sixteen) and what one 1024 px reference costs in input tokens."
  - "Whether the existing sheet palette or the brand tokens lock generated sheets."
  - "Which lane produces the best pose fidelity at 32 px targets; no benchmark exists for a non-human robot at that size."
  - "Retro Diffusion and PixelLab commercial terms and per-call prices, which were not readable from this session."
  - "Whether gpt-image-2's transparent-background preview keeps hard pixel edges or needs alpha thresholding."
  - "What the Claude iOS app actually uses for the Ultracode band; only a logged-in device inspection would settle it."
  - "Whether the per-yawn mascot should be a body or an accessory on the parent in dense holarchy rows, which today render hundreds of unicode marks with no sprite budget."
  - "Whether a user-facing pause toggle belongs in the chrome contract's control slots or reduced motion relies on the OS query alone."

